window.docContents[119].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#chapter-algebraic-graph-theory":{"contents":"variable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\nnamespace SimpleGraph\n\n\nAlgebraic graph theory studies a graph via algebraic objects attached to it, above\nall its automorphism group, which measures how symmetric the graph is, and its\nadjacency matrix, whose powers count walks. The two interact: a permutation of the\nvertices is an automorphism iff it preserves the adjacency matrix.\n\nIn this chapter we start from Mathlib's group structure on the automorphisms G ≃g G\nof a graph, show that isomorphic graphs have isomorphic automorphism groups, and\ncompute the automorphism groups of the infinite path pathNat₁ (trivial) and of the\ncycle graph C[n] (dihedral). We then introduce the adjacency matrix, and show that\nevery vertex of a vertex-transitive graph lies on the same number of closed walks of\neach length, and that isomorphic graphs have the same eigenvalues, though not\nconversely. We also find eigenvalues of Mycielskians, where the golden ratio appears.\nWe survey Mathlib's results on the Laplacian, whose kernel has dimension equal to the\nnumber of connected components, and on strongly regular graphs such as the Petersen\ngraph. We then show that the Hamming cube model hypercubeHamming of the hypercube\nis isomorphic to our recursive box product construction.\n\nThe final three sections are more advanced. Using the Hamming cube model of Q[n],\nwe prove that its automorphism group is the hyperoctahedral group ℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ, of\norder 2 ^ n * n!. We then count copies of one graph in another: the unlabelled\ncopies of K[s] are the s-cliques, and in general the number of labelled copies is\nthe number of unlabelled copies times the number of automorphisms, which we apply to\ncycles. Finally we show that every copy of Q[k] in Q[n] is an induced subcube,\nand count these copies.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean","header":"12. Algebraic Graph Theory","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#chapter-algebraic-graph-theory"},"/Appendix/#section-chapter-8-walks":{"contents":"* Walk.pathNat₁Walk: walk on the infinite path from i to i + j.* Walk.pathGraphWalk: walk along the finite path graph P[n + 1] from 0 to\nFin.last n.* Walk.cycleGraphCycle: walk once around C[n + 3], built by transferring\npathGraphWalk and closing it with the wrap-around edge.* HypercubeVert.hammingPath: path between two hypercube vertices.* HypercubeVert.hammingDist: Hamming distance between two hypercube vertices\n(number of differing coordinates).* HypercubeVert.grayStart / HypercubeVert.grayEnd: start and end vertices of the\nGray code walk.* Walk.grayWalk: Gray code walk on Q[n].* Walk.mycielskianAux: auxiliary zig-zag walk in mμ[G].* Walk.mycielskian: walk in mμ[G] constructed from a walk in G (protected, to\ncoexist with the graph constructor mycielskian).* Walk.mycielskianClosed: closed walk in mμ[G] constructed from a closed walk in\nG (a cycle if the original is one).* Walk.posaRotate: Pósa rotation of a path walk.* Walk.posaClosed: closing a Pósa path to obtain a cycle.* Walk.IsMaximalPath: a maximal path — a path that cannot be extended at either\nend, i.e. every neighbour of either endpoint already lies on it.* IsOrePair: Ore's condition at a pair of vertices — if distinct and non-adjacent,\ntheir degree sum is at least |V|.* IsClosed: a closed graph — one with no eligible pair, i.e. any two distinct\nvertices with degree sum at least |V| are adjacent.* closureStep: one round of the closure process — join every eligible pair at once.* closureAux: fuel-limited iteration of closureStep.* closure: the Bondy–Chvátal closure of a graph (computable).* IsOreGraph: an Ore graph — every pair of vertices satisfies Ore's condition\nIsOrePair.\n\nFrom the exercises:\n\n* Walk.grayCycle: Gray code Hamiltonian cycle in Q[n + 2].* chordedC5: C[5] with the two chords 0 — 2 and 0 — 3, a graph that is not\nan Ore graph but whose closure is complete.* Walk.IsMaximumPath: a maximum-length path — a path such that no path in G is\nlonger.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Definitions","header":"14.2.7. Chapter 8: Walks","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-8-walks"},"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-finiteness-without-data-the-finite-typeclass":{"contents":"Sometimes we know a type is finite but have no canonical enumeration of it, or we\nsimply don't want to drag Fintype data, and the Decidable instances it forces,\nthrough a proof. For this Mathlib provides the Finite typeclass. It tells us that\nthere exist a natural number n and a bijection between the type and Fin n, the\ncanonical type of cardinality n.\n\n#source Finite -- class inductive Finite (α : Sort*) : Prop\n              --    | intro {n : ℕ} : α ≃ Fin n → Finite _\n\n\nexample {α : Type*} : Finite α ↔ ∃ n, Nonempty (α ≃ Fin n) :=\n  finite_iff_exists_equiv_fin\n\n\nThe crucial difference is that Finite V is a proposition whereas Fintype V is\ndata.\n\n* Aspect* Fintype V* Finite V\n* Nature* data (a bundled structure)* a proposition (Prop-valued)\n* Carries* a Finset V of all elements* only ∃ n, Nonempty (V ≃ Fin n)\n* Says* how to enumerate V* that V can be enumerated\n* Two instances* equal, but not always definitionally equal* definitionally equal (proof irrelevance)\n* Computable?* if the instance is (Fintype.card, decide)* no (use Nat.card)\n\nWe always have Fintype V → Finite V; the reverse recovers data from a proof, so it\nneeds the axiom of choice and is noncomputable (but is still very useful in proofs).\n\n#check Finite.of_fintype   -- [Fintype α] : Finite α\n#source Fintype.ofFinite   -- noncomputable def Fintype.ofFinite\n                           --       (α : Type*) [Finite α] : Fintype α\n\n\nSo being data is not the same as being computable. Fintype.ofFinite, like any\ninstance built with the help of the classical tactic, is a perfectly good Fintype\ninstance for use in proofs, but #eval cannot run it.\n\nOne final remark about Fintype instances is that because two instances for the same\ntype need not be definitionally equal, they can be the source of diamonds in\ntypeclass inference. This can make proofs fail in mysterious ways. We will see a\nsimple example of one below in The Handshake Lemma.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees\u0009Finite Types\u0009Cardinality","header":"4.2.4.3. Finiteness Without Data: the  Finite  Typeclass","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-finiteness-without-data-the-finite-typeclass"}});